.TH REPOSE 1 "September 2026" "Repose 0.5-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
repose \- sustained C/C++ repository audits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B repose
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B repose help
.RI [ COMMAND
.RI [ SUBCOMMAND ]]
.br
.B repose
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B repose
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Repose builds a reviewable inventory of a fixed repository snapshot, primarily
for KiCad. Use a clean, dedicated scan checkout with a compilation database
configured for that checkout. This release implements inventory building and
review, clangd semantic indexing, assignment previews, and durable parallel scanning.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR "repose help" " [" COMMAND " [" SUBCOMMAND "]]"
Show categorized global help or focused help for a command or nested subcommand.
The equivalent -h and --help flags can appear anywhere before -- in a command.
.TP
.BR "repose db path" " [--repo " DIR "]"
Print the absolute path of the worktree-local Repose SQLite database. This works
before Repose has created any state and does not open or create the database.
.TP
.BR "repose doctor" " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
Perform a read-only health audit of the Git repository and worktree-local Repose
state. Checks include SQLite integrity and foreign keys, stored inventory hashes
and Git snapshots, current checkout and build-input identity, inventory approval,
semantic-index coverage and its recorded clangd binary, saved scan configuration
and task state, runner executables, reviewer guidance and hints, and repository
model pricing coverage. Optional
or incomplete capabilities are warnings. Corrupt state, missing snapshots or
runners required by unfinished scans, and checkout/build drift are failures and
produce a nonzero exit status. A runner referenced only by historical scans is a
warning. With --json, write the complete report before returning a failed-check error.
.TP
.BR "repose status" " [--scan " ID|latest "] [--jobs " N "] [--json]"
Summarize the current inventory, findings and latest verification outcomes, scan
and assignment states, token usage, and recorded model worker time. Remaining
worker time is estimated from successful assignment durations. --jobs, from 1
through 32, also reports an approximate wall-clock duration at that parallelism;
it does not start or modify a scan. --scan restricts the report to one scan. A
selected recheck uses its original source scan for finding totals. Without
--scan, totals cover all scans and all findings. The command is read-only.
Failed assignments are reported separately and are excluded from remaining-time
estimates until resumed with --retry-failed.
.TP
.BR "repose stats" " [--scan " ID|latest "] [--model " MODEL "] [--since " DATE "] [--json]"
Aggregate retained review and recheck attempts, outcomes, worker time, token
usage, estimated cost, and current finding counts. Failed attempts and retries
remain included. --scan selects one frozen pass. --model and --since filter
attempts; DATE accepts YYYY-MM-DD or RFC3339. Finding counts follow a selected
scan's source but are not filtered by model or date. JSON contains totals and
per-harness/model/effort groups.
.TP
.BR "repose cost" " [--scan " ID|latest "] [--model " MODEL "] [--since " DATE "] [--json]"
Show the accounting-focused form of the stats report. A harness-reported cost
takes precedence; otherwise each attempt is estimated from its own token usage
and the repository model registry. Unknown model pricing and attempts without
accounting data remain explicit. Token-price results are API-equivalent estimates
and do not represent Codex subscription charges.
.TP
.BR "repose model list" " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
List built-in models, repository pricing overrides, and otherwise-unknown model
identifiers observed in saved scans. The text form shows pricing status, origin,
and the number of scans using each exact identifier.
.TP
.BR "repose model show " NAME " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
Show one model's origin, observed scan count, pricing metadata, long-context
threshold, and short- and long-context token rates.
.TP
.BR "repose model set-pricing " NAME " [" OPTIONS "]"
Store repository pricing for an exact model identifier. All of --short-input,
--short-cached-input, --short-cache-write, --short-output, --long-input,
--long-cached-input, --long-cache-write, and --long-output are required and use
USD per million tokens. --service-tier defaults to standard, --source to manual,
--as-of to the current date, and --long-context-threshold to 272000 input tokens.
Configured pricing overrides a built-in entry with the same name and is used by
stats, cost, and doctor. --json writes the resulting registry entry.
.TP
.BR "repose model mark-pricing-unknown " NAME " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
Record that pricing for an exact model identifier is unknown, overriding a
built-in entry with the same name. Existing attempts and harness-reported costs
remain unchanged.
.TP
.BR "repose prompt list" " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
List the effective scan and recheck reviewer instruction sources, active hint
counts, and content identities.
.TP
.BR "repose prompt show" " [--full] " scan|recheck " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
Show the editable reviewer instructions. --full includes active hints and
Repose's fixed read-only, scope, trust-boundary, and response protocol.
.TP
.BR "repose prompt set" " (--file " FILE "|--stdin) " scan|recheck
Save repository reviewer instructions for future work. The editable portion can
change review priorities and project semantics but cannot replace the fixed
protocol. --json reports the resulting effective identity.
.TP
.BR "repose prompt reset " scan|recheck " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
Remove a repository override and restore built-in reviewer instructions. Active
hints remain effective.
.TP
.BR "repose hint add " TEXT " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
Save a project hint with a stable numeric ID. Active hints supplement both scan
and recheck prompts.
.TP
.BR "repose hint list" " [--scan " ID|latest "] [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
List active hints, or the exact hint snapshot frozen in a saved scan.
.TP
.BR "repose hint remove " ID " [--json] [--repo " DIR "]"
Remove an active hint from future work. Saved scan snapshots remain unchanged.
.TP
.B repose scan create --model MODEL
Freeze an approved inventory, selected assignments, prompts, goal, and model
settings. Accepts --inventory ID, --path, --group, --tag, --index, --goal,
--max-files, --max-bytes, --instructions FILE, repeatable --hint, --harness,
--effort, --binary, and --timeout. Defaults to codex/high and a 30 minute
per-assignment timeout.
Gemini requires --effort default. Creation freezes the effective prompt, saved
and one-off hints, project guidance, and prompt identity. Later prompt and hint
edits do not change the scan. Creation makes no model calls.
.TP
.B repose scan run ID
.br
.B repose scan resume [ID]
Execute frozen assignments with --jobs N (default 2, range 1..32).
Resume without an ID selects the only scan with pending or abandoned work in
the checkout; --retry-failed also includes scans with failed assignments.
Completed and invalidated scans are excluded. Multiple candidates are listed
and require an explicit ID. No eligible scans is an error.
--timeout DURATION overrides the saved per-assignment timeout for this invocation,
including retries. It must be positive. Omitting it uses the saved timeout, even
for older scans; overrides do not change the saved default. New attempts record
the effective timeout before dispatch, shown by scan attempts and its JSON export.
--limit N caps total further attempts across all workers, including throttle
retries; --jobs 32 --limit 32 runs one batch of at most 32 attempts.
--duration D stops dispatching after D and
drains active work. Both default to unlimited. --retry-failed retries failures
while retaining prior attempts. Completed assignments are not repeated.
One coordinator runs per checkout. Interrupted or abandoned tasks are recovered
on resume. Changed goals, model settings, or scope require a new scan.
Codex quota exhaustion pauses dispatch and drains active work. Blocked assignments
remain pending and can be resumed when capacity is available, without --retry-failed.
Temporary Codex throttling waits 30s, 1m, then 2m (or a longer provider delay),
draining active workers and using a single probe before restoring parallel dispatch.
After three unsuccessful probes, the scan pauses. Cooldowns survive resume;
all retries count toward --limit. Provider-limit details appear in scan show and
scan attempts. Other harness errors remain explicit failures.
.TP
.B repose scan pause|interrupt ID
Request a running coordinator to drain active work or cancel it, respectively.
Ctrl-C and SIGTERM also cancel active work. Completed results remain saved.
.TP
.B repose scan list|show|tasks|attempts [ID]
List scans, inspect a scan's status, list its tasks, or inspect attempt history.
All except list require a scan ID. --json exports complete records, including
frozen task prompts or raw attempt outputs. Scan IDs accept hexadecimal prefixes
of at least four characters or latest.
.TP
.B repose scan prompt ID TASK
Print a frozen assignment prompt. TASK is an ordinal or assignment ID prefix.
.TP
.B repose findings
Open the findings TUI. --scan ID filters a scan; --all includes dismissed findings;
--json exports instead of opening the terminal. The preview shows source at the
observed snapshot. Use / to search, s/v to filter status/severity, D to dismiss,
r to reopen, n to add a note, t/u to add/remove tags, T to apply exact tag filters,
and R to reload while scanning. V filters the latest
verification verdict; --verification accepts all, unchecked, confirmed,
false_positive, or uncertain. Verdicts and their reasoning appear in the preview
details; prior results remain in the history and JSON export. Repeatable --tag
filters use AND semantics.
.TP
.B repose recheck [FINDING_ID...] --model MODEL
Verify existing findings against their observed snapshot, grouping them by their
original review assignment. --batch-max (default 5) caps findings per model call;
smaller batches remain smaller and never combine different source assignments.
--scan defaults to latest: the newest completed original scan, excluding queued
reviews and recheck passes. An explicit --scan ID can select a partial scan.
Optional finding IDs, --path PREFIX, and repeatable --tag TAG filters narrow scope;
multiple tags use AND semantics and dismissed findings are excluded.
Accepts repeatable --hint, --harness, --effort (default high), --binary,
--timeout (default 30m), --jobs (default 2), --limit, --duration, and
--retry-failed as for scan execution.
Jobs count concurrent batches, limit counts batch attempts including retries,
and timeout applies per batch. --batch-max 1 runs a separate call for each finding.
--dry-run previews without writes or model calls; --create-only saves the queue
and prompts without dispatch. Repeating the same source/selection/model resumes
the matching pass and skips its completed checks. Changing models, selection, or
batch maximum creates a separate pass. --force creates a fresh pass; resume it by
its printed ID. Older single-finding passes still resume by ID with frozen inputs.
Use scan show, tasks, prompt, attempts, pause, interrupt, and resume with the recheck
ID. Quota handling, cooldowns, and attempt caps apply. --timeout explicitly overrides
a resumed pass. Frozen selections include findings later dismissed by the user.
Each finding receives an independent confirmed, false_positive, or uncertain
result with its own reasoning. Missing, duplicate, or malformed results fail the
whole batch without publishing partial verdicts; --retry-failed retries that batch.
Uncertain
completes the check but leaves evidence inconclusive. Results preserve original
findings and manual dispositions; no automatic dismissal occurs. Model identity,
snapshot, raw responses, and verification history remain available for inspection.
.TP
.B repose finding show|dismiss|reopen|note ID
Inspect or triage one finding. Dismiss and note require --reason TEXT.
Findings preserve the observed commit, scan, assignment, attempt, and model
provenance. Recheck verdicts supplement original findings. Automatic consolidation
is not implemented.
.TP
.B repose finding tag|untag ID... --tag TAG [--tag TAG...]
Add or remove one or more tags across any number of findings in one atomic edit.
All IDs and tags are validated before changes are saved. Repeated operations are
idempotent, and actual changes are retained in each finding's history. Tags are
normalized to lowercase; category:value namespacing is supported but optional.
.TP
.B repose tags
List tag counts for open findings. --scan ID|latest restricts the source scan;
--all includes dismissed findings and --json emits structured counts.
.TP
.B repose export --format json|sarif|html [-o FILE]
Export findings as JSON, SARIF, or a self-contained offline HTML viewer.
Accepts --repo DIR, --scan ID|latest, --path PREFIX, --verification VERDICT,
repeatable --tag TAG filters, and --all. Output defaults to stdout;
-o/--output writes to a file relative to the
current directory, and -o - explicitly selects stdout.
JSON/SARIF export open findings unless --all includes dismissed findings. HTML
includes all dispositions and initially shows open findings; --all initially
shows all of them. Its sorting and status/severity/verdict filters work offline,
along with exact tag filters. Selecting multiple tags requires all of them; tags
shown on a finding can also be clicked to filter. Tag counts show how many findings
would remain after adding each tag and respect the other active HTML filters. The
Untagged pseudo-tag selects findings with no tags. Text search covers finding text,
locations, tags, source excerpts, history notes, and recheck reasoning. Multiple
search terms use AND semantics. Command-line path and verdict filters restrict the
embedded records. A URL fragment such as #1234 selects and reveals finding 1234;
mouse and keyboard selection update the fragment, and the detail ID is a link.
Without --scan, findings from every original scan are eligible. Latest selects
the newest completed original scan; a recheck ID selects its source scan's
findings with all their verification history.
All formats retain observed snapshots, assignment/attempt provenance, review model,
manual history, and independent recheck verdicts. JSON has a versioned report with
a findings array; SARIF stores provenance and history in result properties. HTML
shows source excerpts at the observed snapshot, even when the checkout has changed.
Export reads the database without upgrading it or invoking a model.
.TP
.B repose backup [PATH]
Create a consistent, integrity-checked SQLite snapshot of the worktree-local
Repose database. With no path, create
repose-backup-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.sqlite in the current directory, adding a numeric
suffix on collision. The online backup includes committed WAL state and can run
with an active scan. Existing destinations are never overwritten. The resulting
file uses mode 0600. Accepts --repo DIR.
.TP
.B repose backup import [--force] PATH
Validate and import a Repose backup. Integrity, foreign keys, inventory document
hashes, and the availability of recorded Git snapshots are checked before the
worktree-local database is replaced. Existing state requires confirmation unless
--force is supplied. Import is blocked while a scan or semantic index coordinator
is active. The source backup remains unchanged. Accepts --repo DIR.
.TP
.B repose inventory build
Save an immutable inventory of HEAD, build inputs, tracked files, and policy.
Identical inputs reuse an existing version. Inherits the current policy and
compilation database path unless explicitly overridden.
.TP
.B repose inventory list
List inventory versions, newest first, with review status.
.TP
.B repose inventory show [ID]
Show an inventory summary. Defaults to current. With --json, export the full
document including files, compilation commands, and policy.
.TP
.B repose inventory files [ID]
List files, defaulting to in-scope code in the current inventory.
.TP
.B repose inventory tree [ID]
Show directory totals below --path, to --depth levels (default 1). Totals include
all selected descendants. Accepts the same selection filters as files.
.TP
.B repose inventory groups [ID]
Summarize selected files by group, with sizes, coverage gaps, tags and notes.
.TP
.B repose inventory inspect [ID]
Inspect a path/group selection, its coverage gaps, annotations, ten largest
files, and matching policy rules in precedence order.
.TP
.B repose inventory group PREFIX... --name NAME
Assign a group to tracked prefixes, including excluded files. Later overlapping
group rules win. Saves a new current policy version.
.TP
.B repose inventory annotate PREFIX... [--tag TAG] [--note TEXT]
Add tags and a note to prefixes. Repeat --tag for multiple tags. Identical edits
reuse a version. Policy export/import supports removing annotations.
.TP
.B repose inventory plan [ID]
Preview deterministic assignments for included files selected by --path,
--group and --tag. Sorted targets are packed within group/directory boundaries.
Defaults to 8 files and 65536 target bytes per assignment; oversized files remain
flagged singletons. Every selected target appears exactly once. --json exports
inventory/snapshot IDs, goal, selection, limits, target facts and annotations.
This command neither saves a scan nor calls a model or validates the checkout.
Byte counts measure targets, not model context limits. --semantic uses saved
symbols and include context. --tui opens an assignment browser and automatically
uses a matching saved index when available.
.TP
.B repose inventory browse [ID]
Open the terminal inventory browser. Accepts the same selection filters as
files. Current is editable; explicit IDs and latest are read-only. Browsing
uses read-only connections; a writer opens only when saving an edit.
Arrows or j/k navigate, Enter opens, h goes up, d shows details, / filters visible
paths, a toggles all/included files, and r reloads current. Use g for group,
t for tag, n for note, x to exclude, i to include, and p to preview assignments.
Enter submits edits; Escape cancels. Edits affect all paths under the selected
prefix, including hidden files. Concurrent policy changes require a reload and
retry. Press ? for all keys and q to quit.
The s key shows saved semantic facts; p uses semantic planning when a matching
index is available. The r key reloads both policy and indexing results.
In the assignment browser, Tab switches panes; t/s/c/d select target ranges,
symbols, context dependencies, and diagnostics. Enter opens wrapped full details,
/ searches assignments and symbols, and ? shows plan and assignment IDs, goal,
limits, and help. Symbols are restricted to overlapping declarations; enclosing
scopes that extend outside the assignment are labeled. Diagnostics describe
whole-file parsing. Escape returns from details; q returns to the inventory.
.TP
.B repose inventory index [ID]
Index selected C/C++ files using clangd 21+. Uses one shared process, two workers
by default, and a private compilation database. Saves per-file results and
resumes on rerun; interruption retains completed work. The first source command
variant is used. Headers borrow commands from observed includers. Parse errors
remain visible as partial results. Index profiles are independent of policy edits.
Requires a matching clean checkout/build. Only one index coordinator runs per
checkout; inspection and policy editing remain available.
.TP
.B repose inventory index-status [ID]
Show saved semantic coverage, command provenance, and parse errors for the
selection. No compiler or model is started.
.TP
.B repose inventory symbols [ID]
Show saved symbol names, kinds and line numbers. JSON includes exact ranges,
command provenance, includes and diagnostics. Partial results remain labelled.
.TP
.B repose inventory includes [ID]
Show resolved direct include links. Excluded dependencies remain visible as
context. Includes are observations from the indexed configuration, not a complete
transitive graph.
.TP
.B repose inventory check [ID]
Validate the checkout commit, tracked cleanliness, and recorded build inputs.
Defaults to current.
.TP
.B repose inventory approve ID
Record review of a specific inventory after validating its checkout and build
inputs. Requires an explicit ID; current and latest are not accepted. Approval does not run
a scan. New inventory versions require their own review.
.TP
.B repose inventory exclude PREFIX... --reason TEXT
Exclude C/C++ files or directory subtrees from direct review. Derive a new
current inventory from the saved map and persist the policy for future builds.
Existing versions stay unchanged. Excluded code remains available as context.
.TP
.B repose inventory include PREFIX... [--reason TEXT]
Restore C/C++ paths to review scope. The new rule overrides previous scope rules
while preserving grouping annotations.
.TP
.B repose inventory exclusions [ID]
List default and explicit scope rules with matched and effective C/C++ counts.
Defaults to current. Effective counts account for later overrides.
.TP
.B repose inventory policy export [ID]
Export the inventory policy as editable JSON. Defaults to current.
.TP
.B repose inventory policy import FILE
Replace the current policy with a JSON policy and derive an inventory version
without accessing source or build files.
.TP
.B repose version
Print the Repose version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Show global, command, or nested subcommand help without opening repository state.
Use
.B \-\-
to stop help-flag recognition when a positional value begins with a hyphen.
.TP
.B --repo DIR
Dedicated scan checkout, relative to the current directory. Defaults to the
current directory. Available on repository inspection and mutation commands.
.TP
.B --json
Output JSON. Available on inspection and reporting commands where documented.
.TP
.B --compile-commands FILE
Compilation database for inventory build, relative to the checkout.
Initially defaults to build/compile_commands.json; later builds inherit the
current inventory's compilation database path.
.TP
.B --policy FILE
JSON grouping, exclusion, tag, and note rules for inventory build. Relative to
the current directory. Rules match literal repository-relative prefixes;
a dot matches all paths. Replaces the saved policy on successful build.
Explicit imports reject unmatched rules. Inherited rules for paths absent in a
new snapshot are retained and reported.
.TP
.B --reason TEXT
Reason for a scope decision. Required by exclude; optional for include.
.TP
.B --path PREFIX
Filter files, tree, groups, inspect, plan or browse by an exact file or directory prefix.
.TP
.B --group NAME
Filter a selection by group.
.TP
.B --tag TAG
Filter a selection by tag, or add a tag with annotate (repeatable).
.TP
.B --status STATUS
Filter inventory files by included, excluded, all, missing-command, or
header-unmapped. Defaults to included. Filters combine by intersection.
.TP
.B --depth N
Directory levels below --path for tree. Defaults to 1; accepts 0 through 100.
.TP
.B --name NAME
Group name for inventory group.
.TP
.B --note TEXT
Review note to add with inventory annotate.
.TP
.B --goal TEXT
Question for each preview assignment. Defaults to "Find correctness issues."
.TP
.B --max-files N
Maximum targets per preview assignment. Defaults to 8; must be positive.
For index, limits further attempts and defaults to 0 (unlimited).
.TP
.B --max-bytes N
Maximum target file bytes per preview assignment. Defaults to 65536; must be
positive. A larger file stays in a flagged singleton assignment.
.TP
.B --semantic
Use saved symbols and include context for plan. Every selected byte remains
covered once; oversized functions and partial/unindexed files stay intact with
warnings. Ranges are zero-based with exclusive ends. Context paths do not count
toward target limits. No scan is saved or started.
.TP
.B --tui
Open the plan's assignment browser directly. Accepts the same scope, goal, size,
and index options as plan. Uses a matching saved index when available, otherwise
whole-file targets. An explicit --semantic still requires an index. Incompatible
with --json. The displayed plan and semantic results stay fixed until reopened.
On narrow terminals, Tab switches between assignments and their items.
.TP
.B --index ID
Select a semantic profile matching the inventory snapshot/build. Available on
index-status, symbols, includes and plan; implies --semantic for plan. Defaults
to the latest matching profile, including an index still building.
.TP
.B --clangd FILE
Clangd executable for index; defaults to clangd. Requires version 21 or newer.
.TP
.B --jobs N
Concurrent index workers; defaults to 2, accepts 1 through 32.
.TP
.B --timeout DURATION
Initialization and per-file indexing timeout; defaults to 2m.
.TP
.B --retry-errors
Retry partial, failed and unavailable index results.
.TP
.B --refresh
Reparse selected index files while preserving older results. Use after changes
to transitive generated dependencies, SDKs or system libraries. Direct and
explicit forced-include fingerprints are checked automatically on rerun.
.SH INVENTORY IDS
Use a full ID or an unambiguous hexadecimal prefix of at least four characters.
Inventory content is immutable. Rebuilding after changing policy creates a new
version. Approval and timestamps are separate metadata.
Inspection defaults to current, the version selected by the last successful
build or policy edit. The latest selector names the most recently created
version, which can differ after restoring an earlier policy.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <worktree-git-dir>/repose/repose.sqlite
Worktree-local inventory storage, separate from AIR and other linked worktrees.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Untracked files are outside the source inventory. Generated and external
compilation inputs are counted separately. Header parsing uses borrowed source
commands and can be incomplete when include ordering matters. Only one source
command variant is indexed; full reference graphs are not yet available.
The build input checks do not establish freshness of generated headers,
external dependencies, compiler binaries, or an index.
Inspection uses read-only database connections without schema migration.
Writers retain WAL/SHM sidecars for subsequent read-only access. Older databases
without those sidecars require one writable open first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR cmake (1),
.BR clangd (1)
